| 4. Mechanism | **With effort.** The paragraph gives the court’s distinction and the parole alternative. Ask for one plain sentence connecting the statutory label to his presence inside the country, in place of the opening exceptions. |
| 5. What changed | **Not as marked.** The marked paragraph gives the 1997 rule and rehearing dispute. The early dated passage carries the intervening change, Board decision, appeal and grant. Keep that sequence; trim the later retelling. |
| 6. Unresolved | **Yes.** It separates this statutory grant from due process and says no argument day is set. The other petitions’ procedural details obscure that answer. |

## The stranger’s read

I take the report in its order. “Act” identifies the edit or hole below. Repeated stalls are decided here.

| Stranger’s item | Call |
| --- | --- |
| 1. What happened | **Leave:** the opening states the grant and its limit. |
| 2. Why it matters | **Act, edit 4:** break up the early arrest-to-release sequence so its consequence can be retained. |
| 3. Wrong reading | **Act, edit 7 and hole 1:** make the CBS figure visibly an outlet claim. |
| 4. Mechanism | **Act, edit 3 and hole 2:** replace the opening exceptions with the usable distinction. |
| 5. What changed | **Leave:** the dated early passage carries it. |
| 6. Unresolved | **Leave:** “No argument date has been set” is repeatable. |
| 7. Width of the split | **Act, edit 6:** remove the intervening qualification; the nine-to-two result can stand on its own. |
| July 8 timeline row: which opinion? | **Act, edit 9:** the table does not need a row whose action is that an opinion cites guidance. |
| “Executive Branch practice” | **Act, edit 10:** the dek and early passage already name the bond practice. |
| Port-of-entry wording | **Leave:** the next sentence supplies its purpose by contrasting the notice with an arrest years after entry. |
