checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:5:- **Major, grade A: The legal hinge.** The page says the Court will decide whether the statute requires holding people “who are in the United States and were not admitted.” The dispute turns on two different phrases: **“applicant for admission”** and **“seeking admission.”** The [Eighth Circuit opinion](https://ecf.ca8.uscourts.gov/opndir/26/03/253248P.pdf) calls their relationship “the central inquiry”; the [Fourth Circuit opinion](https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/257044.P.pdf) explains that its petitioners contested the second condition. The correct account is that the government treats the phrases as equivalent, while the Second Circuit held that someone arrested in the interior long after entry is not then seeking admission.
