checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r3c-editor.md:67:1. **Minor, new. TIGHTEN:** “For about thirty years, people arrested inside the country who had entered without inspection and had not been admitted or paroled could ask an immigration judge for release on bond, unless they were apprehended while entering or shortly afterward or held on a criminal or terrorism ground.” The early sequence repeats the practice and its limits.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r3c-editor.md:77:6. **Minor, residual. CUT:** “The Second Circuit's own holding covers people who entered without inspection and were not apprehended while entering or shortly afterward.” The mechanism paragraph immediately above gives this limit. Removing it also lets the split follow the person’s stakes without interruption.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r3-stranger.md:3:1. What happened: **Answered, no work.** "On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court agreed to decide whether the government must hold, while their removal cases are pending, people who are living in the United States and were never admitted."
