242:We find merit to both interpretations. In contrast to the statutorily defined term “admission,” the phrase “applicant for admission” is not included in the INA’s definitional section. Compare 8 U.S.C. § 1101(a)(13) with 8 U.S.C. § 1225(a)(1). Moreover, when Congress defines a term of art, it generally uses words like “means” or “is” to signal that it is doing so. United States v. Helton, 944 F.3d 198, 206 n.5 (4th Cir. 2019); see also Helvering v. Morgan’s Inc., 293 U.S. 121, 125 n.1 (1934) (“The natural distinction would be that where ‘means’ is employed, the term and its definition are to be interchangeable equivalents[.]”). By contrast, Section 1225(a)(1) uses the word “deemed,” suggesting that the intention was, in fact, to deem. The statute says that both noncitizens arriving in the United States and those present in the United States without being admitted “shall be deemed” applicants for admission. 8 U.S.C. § 1225(a)(1) (emphasis added). The title of Section 1225(a)(1), “Aliens treated as applicants for admission,” also signals as much. Id. (emphasis added). Certainly, referring to noncitizens, like the Petitioners, who have lived in our country for decades and are not in fact applying for admission, as “applicants for admission” suggests the creation of a legal fiction, as one would expect a deeming provision to do.
