264-Not all references to “active service” in the Health Professions Education Partnerships Act, however, refer to those in the Public Health Service. In interpreting the statute, the Hedin court noted that, where the statute sought to “address[ ] a certain kind of ‘active service,’ including military-type service, it expressly modifies the term ‘active service’ to indicate this limitation.” 355 F.3d at 749. For example, where the statute provides for “active service in time of war,” the deeming provision does not apply. Id. (quoting 42 U.S.C. § 213(a)). Nor does the legal fiction expand to encompass qualities of traditional military service. Where, for instance, “active service” is modified to add “in such grade or any higher grade in the Reserve Corps,” the legal fiction does not extend to members of the Public Health Service. Id. (quoting 42 U.S.C. § 211(j)(2)). Similarly, the phrase “applicant for admission” in Section 1225(b)(2)(A) is modified to add “seeking admission.” Applying the reasoning of Hedin, even if we were to accept that the phrase is a deeming provision, it would not follow that the Petitioners are seeking admission. Thus, though Section 1226(a) unambiguously applies to the Petitioners, we are confronted with two layers of ambiguity in Section 1225(b)(2)(A). First, whether the phrase, “applicant for admission,” is a definitional or deeming provision. Second, assuming it is a deeming provision, the impact of the modifying phrase, “seeking admission.” The Government asks us to read “an alien seeking admission” as synonymous with “applicant for admission.” The Petitioners counter that “seeking admission” must be read to apply
