278-B. Giving Effect to Each Word It is a “cardinal principle of statutory construction that we must give effect, if possible, to every clause and word of a statute.” Williams v. Taylor, 529 U.S. 362, 404 (2000) (quoting United States v. Menasche, 348 U.S. 528, 538–39 (1955)). This canon is known as the canon against surplusage. It requires us to construe a statute “so that no part will be inoperative or superfluous, void or insignificant.” Corley v. United States, 556 U.S. 303, 314 (2009) (quoting Hibbs v. Winn, 542 U.S. 88, 101 (2004)). Though this canon is not “an iron rule,” Mullin v. Al Otro Lado, 609 U.S. ---, 146 S. Ct. 2079, 2092 (2026), where, as here, there are multiple possible interpretations of a statute and one would avoid creating surplusage, the canon is “strongest.” Marx v. Gen. Revenue Corp., 568 U.S. 371, 386 (2013). The Government’s interpretation of Section 1225(b)(2)(A) collapses two distinct phrases, “applicant for admission” and “seeking admission,” into one. If that construction were to hold water—that an applicant for admission is necessarily seeking admission— there would no reason to include the phrase “seeking admission.” It would be pure surplusage. Had Congress not included that phrase, then the plain text of Section 1225(b)(2)(A) would support the Government’s position. The provision would read as follows: Mandatory detention is required in the case of any “applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that [the] alien seeking admission is not clearly
279-
280---- page 31 ---
281-31
282-
283-and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.” This is not what the statute says, however. The flaw in the Government’s construction is thus evident; it requires us to write an entire phrase—seeking admission—out of the sentence. See United States v. Taylor, 596 U.S. 845, 857 (2022) (“[W]e do not lightly assume Congress adopts two separate clauses in the same law to perform the same work.”). The Petitioners’ reading, by contrast, gives meaning to each word in Section 1225(b)(2)(A). This corresponds with another commonly applied canon of statutory construction: the meaningful variation canon. Generally, where Congress uses different terms, it “suggests a variation in meaning.” Antonin Scalia & Bryan A. Garner, Reading Law: The Interpretation of Legal Texts 170 (2012). Where a statute employs “one term in one place, and a materially different term in another, the presumption is that the different term denotes a different idea.” Valladares, 130 F.4th at 82 (quoting S.W. Airlines Co. v. Saxon, 596 U.S. 450, 457–58 (2012)). Applying this canon, an “applicant for admission” is not necessarily “seeking admission.” Under the Petitioners’ reading, “seeking admission” describes the active and ongoing conduct of certain applicants for admission: those physically attempting to come into the United States, typically at a border or port of entry, and presenting themselves for inspection and authorization to lawfully enter.10 While
